*** ANTES DE C++11

Antes de C++11 se solia hacer con punteros o referencias, pero si se queria 
thread safety se requeria de mutex explicitos como se explica aqui:
https://refactoring.guru/design-patterns/singleton/cpp/example#example-1

Una implementacion no thread-safe es:

class Singleton
{

    /**
     * The Singleton's constructor should always be private to prevent direct
     * construction calls with the `new` operator.
     */

protected:
    Singleton(){}

    static Singleton* singleton_;

public:

    /**
     * Singletons should not be cloneable.
     */
    Singleton(Singleton &other) = delete;
    /**
     * Singletons should not be assignable.
     */
    void operator=(const Singleton &) = delete;
    /**
     * This is the static method that controls the access to the singleton
     * instance. On the first run, it creates a singleton object and places it
     * into the static field. On subsequent runs, it returns the client existing
     * object stored in the static field.
     */
    static Singleton *GetInstance(const std::string& value);
};

Singleton* Singleton::singleton_= nullptr;

/**
 * Static methods should be defined outside the class.
 */
Singleton *Singleton::GetInstance()
{
    /**
     * This is a safer way to create an instance. instance = new Singleton is
     * dangeruous in case two instance threads wants to access at the same time
     */
    if(singleton_==nullptr){
        singleton_ = new Singleton();
    }
    return singleton_;
}


*** A PARTIR DE C++11

Desde c++11 las variables static dentro de funciones son thread-safe 
implicitamente, lo que las hace muy convenientes para implementar un singleton.
El Meyers' Singleton hace uso de esta caracteristica del lenguaje:

class MeyerSingleton {
public:
    // Provides global access to the singleton instance.
    static MeyerSingleton& instance() {
        // This static local variable is created on first use and is thread-safe
        static MeyerSingleton instance;
        
        return instance;
    }

    // Delete copy constructor and assignment operator to prevent copies.
    MeyerSingleton(const MeyerSingleton&) = delete;
    MeyerSingleton& operator=(const MeyerSingleton&) = delete;

private:
    // Private constructor prevents direct instantiation.
    MeyerSingleton() {}
    
    // Private destructor can be defined if special cleanup is needed.
    ~MeyerSingleton() = default;
};

int main() {
    // Accessing the singleton instance.
    MeyerSingleton::instance();
    
    // Further calls return the same instance.
    MeyerSingleton::instance();

    return 0;
}
